    "title": "Context7 in the workflow",
    "html": "<p align=\"center\">English | <a href=\"context7.pt-br.md\">Português (Brasil)</a></p><h1>Context7 in the workflow</h1>\n<p>During tutorial <a href=\"/docs/getting-started/03-trabalhando-com-ia\">03</a>, when the\nAI was writing the slot-confirmation integration with the payment gateway,\nit needed the gateway&#39;s CURRENT API — not the API from when its model was\ntrained. That gap is what the Context7 integration exists for.</p>\n<h2>The trigger</h2>\n<p>The operation rule lives at <code>profile/context7.md</code> in the config (the shelf\nof <a href=\"/docs/concepts/profile\">profile</a>): <strong>BEFORE answering about a\nlibrary/framework/SDK/API/CLI/cloud, the agent consults Context7</strong> — it\nresolves the library ID and fetches the current documentation through the\nMCP tool. Model memory ages; the source&#39;s documentation doesn&#39;t.</p>\n<h2>How the kit delivers it</h2>\n<p>The <code>golmet install</code> wizard registers the Context7 MCP tool in the engine\n(the catalog marks the integration), and the guided setup (<code>gm-setup</code>,\noptional) validates it with a smoke test. The API key is optional (free at\ncontext7.com) — via the instance&#39;s <code>profile/</code>, never in the repo.</p>\n<h2>The boundary</h2>\n<p>Context7 is public documentation research — product data (the org, tokens,\nrequirements) never goes into the query. In MeuApp, the AI looked up how the\ngateway confirms payments; HOW MeuApp confirms the slot is in RN-001 — one\nis the world&#39;s API, the other is your product&#39;s rule.</p>\n",
