    "slug": "concepts/organization",
    "base": "concepts/organization",
    "locale": "en",
    "lang": "en",
    "group": "Concepts",
    "title": "Organization, workspaces and versioning",
    "html": "<p align=\"center\">English | <a href=\"organization.pt-br.md\">Português (Brasil)</a></p><h1>Organization, workspaces and versioning</h1>\n<p>When the operator created MinhaOrganizacao in tutorial\n<a href=\"/docs/getting-started/01-organizacao\">01</a>, Golmet asked &quot;what do you want\nto initialize?&quot; — and the journey&#39;s first choice defines the structure of\neverything: <strong>the Organization is the root that groups the workspaces</strong>, it\nkeeps the principles (<code>CONSTITUTION.md</code>), the navigation (<code>INDEX.md</code>) and\nthe product manifest (<code>products:</code>). It doesn&#39;t run the requirements\npipeline — the pipeline lives in the workspaces.</p>\n<h2>The two versioning models</h2>\n<p>The decision that shapes every product is the <code>versioning:</code> field in the\norg&#39;s golmet.yaml. MinhaOrganizacao uses <code>org-repo</code>: <strong>the whole organization is\none Git repository</strong> — MeuApp is a folder versioned inside it. In this\nmodel, the <code>.gitignore</code> belongs to the org root (created by <code>init --org</code>),\nthe kit zone (<code>.golmet/</code>) belongs to the root — shared — and the product is\nborn lean, without its own zone and without its own <code>.gitignore</code>: the\norg&#39;s versioning already covers it.</p>\n<p>The other model, <code>produtos-repo</code>, is the FORMAT classic (DR-008): each\nproduct is its own repository, with its own versioned <code>.golmet/</code> (the kit\nof THAT repo, inherited by the product team when cloning) and its own\n<code>.gitignore</code>. Containers created before the field existed are treated as\n<code>produtos-repo</code> (the retroactive default).</p>\n<p>In practice: <code>init --into</code> reads the declared model and delivers the\nproduct the right way, reporting which one it applied — &quot;organization\nversioning model: org-repo&quot;. The <code>.golmet/</code> zone changes role by context\n(workspace versioned · engine config state · container work zone) — the\ndetail in <a href=\"/docs/concepts/layers\">layers.md</a>.</p>\n<h2>The catalog and the truth</h2>\n<p>The organizations the kit creates (or adopts) enter the <strong>global catalog</strong>\n(<code>~/.golmet/organizations.json</code>) — the wizard uses the catalog to list orgs\nwhen someone creates a product. The invariant: <strong>the org&#39;s golmet.yaml is\nthe source of truth</strong> (structure and <code>products:</code>); the catalog is\nconvenience. When the validate runs on a container, it validates the\ndeclaration: a declared product without a directory is a failure; a product\ndirectory outside products is a warning — the org and the disk must tell\nthe same story.</p>\n<h2>The scoping rule</h2>\n<p>A practical question the structure answers: <strong>where do I write this\nartifact?</strong> The rule is one: in the subject&#39;s workspace. A product subject\n(an RN, a spec) is written in the product workspace; a transversal org\nsubject (a meeting checkpoint, a migration history) is written in the\norg&#39;s work zone <code>.golmet/</code> — gitignored, because it&#39;s kit work, not\ncompany content. And what fits nowhere is born in the work zone and\npromoted by decision — never loose at the root.</p>\n",
    "translated": true
